OneiOS (fix/s24-fps-heat-camera, 2026-11-25): Turnip's Darwin client ends a kernel submission only where a command
buffer begins. Ported from Droid-VM/UniXNU fb9fb47 (patches/mesa/turnip-darwin-submit-split-at-command-buffers.patch,
BSD-2-Clause-Patent, UniXNU contributors; the file it patches is Mesa's, MIT), rebased onto our staged tree (the same
hunks, 38 lines further down after trace-turnip-init.patch and turnip-idle-bo-cache.patch). Applied after
turnip-idle-bo-cache.patch.

darwin_queue_submit() hands the kernel at most NativeAdrenoAbiMaxBuffers (128) buffers a submission and cut a longer
list after every 128, sometimes inside one command buffer. That later chunk then began after the kernel's own ring
commands (CP_THREAD_CONTROL BOTH, concurrent binning on), with no thread control of its own: BV ran it as well as BR,
stopped at its first cache event or blit, and BR waited for it ("gmu: GPU hang detected", KGSL skips the submission,
the guest stalls 1-2 s). Droid-VM saw it at 5-10% of backboardd's starts (its first frames submit a few hundred
buffers); 0 in 80 restarts after. Now a submission ends before a buffer whose first packet is a CP_THREAD_CONTROL (how
every command buffer begins on a7xx, tu_init_hw); a run of 128 with no such beginning is cut as before, warned once:
"darwin: N command buffers' streams with no beginning among them: a kernel submission ends inside one".

